WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:06.250
Leah: Quite a lot of the time the focus
of effective teamwork relates to have good

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:11.160
working relationships with other members
of the team and while this is critical

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:16.129
to the successful completion of projects,
what about relationships with other teams?

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.690
Chris: It is just as important to
develop good working relationships

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:25.250
with other teams as there are very few
instances when a project relies solely

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.960
on the input and output of one team.

00:00:28.639 --> 00:00:32.209
To successfully complete a project
there will likely be several teams

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:33.830
working on different elements.

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:39.070
Leah: As we mentioned in session 1,
when working in an ‘Agile’ environment,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.960
projects will involve teams with a
variety of skillsets who will work

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.870
on different elements of a project.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:50.430
These teams will rely on each other as
the project progresses and frequently

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.610
use ‘Scrum’ and ‘Kanban’ methodologies
to track progress across the teams.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.960
This enables the teams to
monitor progress and identify any

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:04.459
potential risks or holdups and
develop solutions to move forward.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:09.349
Chris: Multi team projects can help
to maintain effective and develop

00:01:09.349 --> 00:01:11.229
positive working relationships.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:16.380
This way you can gain a holistic view of
the project and working with other teams

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.910
on collaborative projects are also a
useful way to gain an understanding about

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:26.040
the organisation’s other moving parts,
and how those parts can work together to

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.170
better achieve the organisation’s mission.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.320
When members of different teams
communicate frequently, you should

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.320
always feel able to ask questions
and have a firm grasp of the nature

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:41.109
of one another’s work and therefore
productivity is considerably boosted.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.490
Leah: Collaborative work also allows
team members to bring their individual

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:50.460
work styles, skills, and perspectives
to projects which creates opportunities

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.460
for new solutions and better results.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:58.449
Imagine not knowing that the perfect
approach, team member, or resource

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:02.759
for a task your team is stuck on, has
been under your nose the entire time.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.910
Chris: Good communication
provides opportunities to improve

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:08.690
your own teams’ methodologies.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.950
When teams from different departments
are transparent about how they work,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.860
it gives you the opportunity to
review the way you do your own work

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:21.320
which can highlight any weaknesses
that you or another team might have.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.100
When working together, take note of
what questions others ask about your

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.710
department, what is confusing to them
about the way you do your work, and

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:35.510
consider how that might help you to find
ways to improve your own ways of working.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.030
Leah: It can also help you to gain a
greater sense of the challenges that

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:44.480
might be faced in terms of deadlines and
discover ways you can continue to support

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.780
one another, coordinate your work with
each other, and provide support to others.

